# Golden vectors for the Modbus TCP worked example.
#
# Built by pymodbus 3.8.6 rather than by hand: each PDU is what
# `pdu.encode()` produced and each header is the first seven bytes of what
# `FramerSocket.buildFrame` put in front of it. pymodbus is what the Python
# side of this protocol is actually tested against, so these are bytes an
# independent implementation considers well formed.
#
#   framer = FramerSocket(DecodePDU(False))
#   pdu = ReadHoldingRegistersRequest(address=0x006B, count=3, dev_id=17)
#   pdu.transaction_id = 1
#   framer.buildFrame(pdu)   ->  00 01 00 00 00 06 11 03 00 6B 00 03
#
# The read-holding pair is also the worked example the specification itself
# gives -- registers 107 to 109, answering AE41 5652 4340 -- so the one case
# a reader is most likely to check by eye agrees with the document as well as
# with the library.
#
# Three structs because the schema splits the frame three ways: the MBAP
# header is its own fixed seven bytes, and `request` and `response` are the
# same function code over two different layouts, which is the point that
# schema makes. The vectors are therefore the header and the PDUs separately
# rather than whole frames, since no struct here spans both.

# The MBAP header, taken off the front of the framed read-holding request.
mbap_header read_holding 00 01 00 00 00 06 11

# Requests: the four-code read shape, both single writes, and the multiple.
request read_coils      01 00 13 00 25
request read_holding    03 00 6B 00 03
request write_register  06 00 01 00 03
request write_registers 10 00 01 00 02 04 00 0A 01 02

# A response: the same code, the other layout -- a byte count and the data.
response read_holding   03 06 AE 41 56 52 43 40
